// Boson sampling on 3 modes with 2 photons - the case where the PERMANENT
// actually carries the physics.
//
// The HOM dip and the MZI are both solvable by hand, which is what makes them
// good first tests and poor last ones: a 2x2 permanent is just ad - bc with a
// plus sign, so an implementation could get the general permanent badly wrong
// and still pass both. This circuit uses an unbalanced, asymmetric mesh so that
// several output configurations have genuinely different amplitudes, and the
// 2-photon amplitudes are permanents of 2x2 SUBMATRICES chosen by the output
// occupation - which is the code path boson sampling actually exercises.
//
// Run it with aria:
//   omega-run examples/circuits/boson_sampling.opticqasm \
//     --backend photonics --input 1,1,0
//
// Run the SAME circuit through Perceval (Quandela) - see
// docs/PHOTONICS_EXAMPLES.md for the full recipe and the convention mapping.
// The short version, given the bridge is installed:
//   printf '{"qasm":%s,"shots":200000,"input_fock":[1,1,0]}' \
//     "$(python3 -c 'import json,sys;print(json.dumps(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))' \
//        examples/circuits/boson_sampling.opticqasm)" \
//     | crates/omega-bridges/python/omega-bridge-perceval-runner
//
// Photon number is conserved, so the only reachable outputs are the six
// 2-photon configurations over 3 modes: |2,0,0> |0,2,0> |0,0,2> |1,1,0>
// |1,0,1> |0,1,1>. Their probabilities must sum to 1 - a cheap invariant that
// catches a mis-indexed submatrix immediately, since a wrong column selection
// almost never sums back to unity.
//
// Conventions are the ones documented in hom_dip.opticqasm: the beam splitter
// is the Rx form and `ps` applies e^{i*phi} to its mode. Perceval's BS uses a
// half-angle, so theta_perceval = 2 * theta_omega; that factor is applied in
// perceval_runner.py and is exactly the kind of mapping this example is here to
// keep honest.

OPTICQASM 1.0;
photon q[3];

// Deliberately NOT balanced: theta = 0.6 gives a ~68/32 split, so transmission
// and reflection carry different weights and a swapped pair of matrix entries
// changes the answer. At 50/50 it often would not.
bs_rx(0.6, 0.0) q[0], q[1];

// A non-zero transverse phase on the second splitter. With phi_tr = 0
// everywhere the mesh stays real, and a dropped complex conjugate anywhere in
// the permanent would go unnoticed.
bs_rx(0.9, 0.4) q[1], q[2];

// Phase on the middle mode, so the two interfering paths through mode 1 pick up
// a relative phase rather than merely a magnitude.
ps(1.1) q[1];

// Final mixing back across the first pair, making every output mode reachable
// from every input mode.
bs_rx(0.35, -0.7) q[0], q[1];
